{
  "title": "Stop appending ', presumably Diamond' to kosher salt ingredient \
names",
  "description": "The ingredient normalizer rewrites raw 'kosher salt' entries \
into item names like 'kosher salt, presumably Diamond', guessing a brand the \
source never mentioned.\n\
\n\
Example from \
data/pipeline-snapshots/smittenkitchen-com_2009-09-grilled-lamb-kebabs-tzatziki.json: \
the raw text '1 teaspoon kosher salt' becomes `item: 'kosher salt, presumably \
Diamond'`. Both kosher-salt entries in that file have the suffix injected. The \
source page (cached at \
~/.ramekin/http-cache/smittenkitchen-com_2009-09-grilled-lamb-kebabs-tzatziki/response.bin) \
makes no mention of Diamond.\n\
\n\
This is intentional behavior but it's not pulling weight. Users who care about \
the Diamond-vs-Morton distinction already know Diamond is the implicit default \
in US recipes; users who don't care find the injected hedge noisy and \
confusing. Just drop the suffix -- leave the item as 'kosher salt'.",
  "status": "open",
  "priority": 3,
  "type": "task",
  "labels": [
    "ingredient-parser",
  ],
  "created_at": "2026-04-20T09:33:56-07:00",
  "updated_at": "2026-04-20T09:33:56-07:00",
}
